/* data/learn-c/chem-12-04.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-04.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-04"] = {"4.1":{"s":"Thermodynamics studies energy changes in physical and chemical processes. It tells whether a change can happen and how much heat or work is involved, but not how fast it happens (that is kinetics).","x":["Thermodynamics studies how energy changes from one form to another, and in particular how heat and work are linked. For chemistry it answers two practical questions. How much heat does a reaction give out or take in? And will a reaction happen by itself?","It deals with large collections of molecules, so it looks at bulk properties like temperature, pressure and volume, and it does not say how fast a reaction goes. That is the job of chemical kinetics, the next chapter but one.","The chapter builds up in steps: the language (system, surroundings, state functions), the first law and enthalpy, thermochemistry (heats of reaction and Hess's law), and then the second law with entropy and Gibbs energy to decide whether a change is spontaneous."],"e":"Burning petrol gives out a lot of heat, so thermodynamics says it is energetically favourable. It does not start by itself at room temperature, because the speed is a question of kinetics."},"4.2":{"s":"The system is the part we study; everything else is the surroundings. Systems are open, closed or isolated. State functions (U, H, S, G) depend only on the present state; path functions (heat, work) depend on how the change is made. Processes are isothermal, adiabatic, isobaric, isochoric, reversible or irreversible.","w":["Heat and work are path functions, not state functions."],"x":["The system is the part of the universe we study, for example the reacting mixture in a flask. The surroundings are everything else that can exchange energy with it. The boundary separates them.","Systems are of three kinds. An open system exchanges both matter and energy with its surroundings (an open beaker of boiling water). A closed system exchanges energy but not matter (a sealed flask). An isolated system exchanges neither (an ideal thermos flask).","The state of a system is described by properties such as pressure P, volume V, temperature T and amount n. A property that depends only on the present state is a state function: internal energy U, enthalpy H, entropy S and Gibbs energy G. Its change depends only on the initial and final states, not on the path.","Properties are extensive if they depend on the amount of matter (mass, volume, energy) and intensive if not (temperature, pressure, density). A process can be isothermal (constant T), isobaric (constant P), isochoric (constant V) or adiabatic (no heat exchanged)."],"e":"Climbing a hill from the same base to the same top by two different paths gives the same change in height (a state function). The length of the walk is different (like heat and work, which are path functions)."},"4.3":{"s":"Work and heat are two ways of exchanging energy. The work in chemistry is mostly pressure-volume work: a gas that expands does work on the surroundings. Sign convention: energy entering the system is positive, energy leaving is negative.","w":["Work done BY the system is negative and heat released is negative in this book's convention."],"x":["Energy can enter or leave a system in two ways. Heat q is energy that flows because of a temperature difference, from the hotter body to the colder one. Work w is energy transferred by a force moving something, for example a gas pushing a piston.","Heat and work are not properties of the system. They are ways of transferring energy, and their amounts depend on the path. A gas can go from the same initial state to the same final state with different amounts of heat and work in each case.","The sign convention (IUPAC) takes the point of view of the system. Heat absorbed by the system is positive (q greater than zero), and heat given out is negative. Work done on the system is positive, and work done by the system on the surroundings is negative.","The unit for both is the joule (J). Heat is also measured in calories, where 1 cal = 4.184 J."],"e":"A gas that absorbs 100 J of heat from a hot plate and pushes out a piston, doing 30 J of work, has q = +100 J and w = −30 J."},"4.4":{"s":"When a gas expands against a constant outside pressure, the work is the product of that pressure and the change in volume, with a minus sign for the system. In a free expansion (against zero pressure) no work is done. Pressure in bar and volume in dm³ give work in units of 100 J.","f":[{"x":"W = -P_ext*Delta V","n":"Pressure-volume work done on the system; 1 dm^3 bar = 100 J.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.4, p.68"}],"w":["Expansion has ΔV > 0, so W < 0: the system loses energy by doing work."],"x":["A common type of work in chemistry is pressure–volume work done when a gas expands or is compressed. Imagine a gas in a cylinder with a piston of area A, with external pressure P_ext pressing on the piston. If the piston moves by a distance d, the volume changes by ΔV = Ad and the work is P_ext × A × d = P_extΔV.","For the system, w = −P_extΔV. When the gas expands (ΔV positive), w is negative: the system does work. When it is compressed, ΔV is negative and w is positive: work is done on the system. In a free expansion into a vacuum, P_ext = 0, so w = 0.","The unit works out as pascal × m³ = joule. If P is in litre atm, the work is in litre atm, and 1 L atm = 101.3 J.","For a reaction at constant pressure that produces gas, the gas pushes back the atmosphere, so some of the energy is lost as work. This is the reason why ΔH and ΔU differ."],"e":"A gas expands from 1.0 L to 3.0 L against a constant 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa. The work done is −2.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = −400 J."},"4.5":{"s":"The work done by a gas is largest in size when the expansion is reversible: the outside pressure is always only infinitesimally smaller than the gas pressure, so the process takes infinitely many steps. For an ideal gas at constant temperature this maximum work depends on the ratio of the final to the initial volume.","f":[{"x":"W_max = -2.303*n*R*T*log_10(V_2/V_1)","n":"Maximum (reversible, isothermal) work on expansion of n moles of ideal gas: negative for expansion in the book's sign convention (work done by the system); also = -n R T ln(V_2/V_1).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.5, p.69"}],"w":["Only a reversible process gives the maximum work."],"x":["How much work you get from an expanding gas depends on how it expands. If the external pressure is always a bit less than the gas pressure, the change takes place in a very large number of tiny steps. This is a reversible process: at every stage the system is in equilibrium, and the process can be reversed by a tiny change in the conditions.","A reversible expansion makes the gas do the maximum possible work, because the external pressure is as high as it can be at every moment. A sudden expansion against a low constant pressure gives much less work. A free expansion gives none.","For n moles of an ideal gas expanding reversibly and isothermally from V₁ to V₂, the work done by the gas is nRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 2.303 nRT log(V₂/V₁). Since the temperature is constant, ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas, so the heat absorbed equals the work done.","Real processes are irreversible, and they take place in a finite time with a finite difference of pressure or temperature. The reversible process is an ideal limit that tells us the best that can be done."],"e":"1 mol of an ideal gas at 300 K doubling its volume reversibly does 2.303 × 8.314 × 300 × log 2 = 1729 J of work, the most it can do for that change."},"4.6":{"s":"Internal energy U is the total energy of a system: the energy of motion and interactions of all its molecules. It is a state function, and its absolute value cannot be measured, only its change. It rises when heat is absorbed or work is done on the system.","x":["The internal energy U of a system is the total energy of all its molecules: the kinetic energy of their motion (translation, rotation, vibration) and the potential energy from the forces between them. It does not include the energy of the system as a whole (for instance, moving in space).","We cannot measure the absolute value of U, only its change ΔU. U is a state function and it is extensive. For an ideal gas, U depends only on temperature, because there are no forces between the molecules.","The internal energy changes in two ways: by exchanging heat with the surroundings and by work done on or by the system. If the system is isolated, U stays the same. In a process at constant volume no pressure–volume work is done, so the heat absorbed is equal to the change in internal energy: q_V = ΔU.","When a system gives energy to the surroundings by heat or work, its internal energy falls. That is why a gas cools down when it expands quickly and does work."],"e":"A gas has U₁ = 500 J. It takes in 200 J of heat and has 50 J of work done on it, so U₂ = 500 + 200 + 50 = 750 J."},"4.7":{"s":"The first law is conservation of energy: the energy of an isolated system is constant. The change in internal energy of a closed system equals the heat absorbed plus the work done on it. Energy can change form but is neither created nor destroyed.","f":[{"x":"Delta U = Q + W","n":"First law: Q = heat supplied to the system, W = work done ON the system (this book's sign rule); both positive when energy enters the system.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.7, p.71"},{"x":"W = -Q","n":"Isothermal process of an ideal gas: Delta U = 0, so the work done on the system equals the heat given out.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.7, p.71"},{"x":"Delta U = W","n":"Adiabatic process: no heat is exchanged (Q = 0), so the change in internal energy is the work done on the system.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.7, p.71"}],"w":["In ΔU = Q + W (this book's sign rule), W is the work done ON the system."],"x":["The first law of thermodynamics is the law of conservation of energy: energy can be changed from one form to another but cannot be created or destroyed. For a closed system, the change in internal energy is the sum of the heat added to the system and the work done on it: ΔU = q + w.","Look at some cases. Heat added at constant volume (w = 0): ΔU = q_V. Isothermal change of an ideal gas (ΔU = 0): q = −w. Adiabatic change (q = 0): ΔU = w, so a gas that expands adiabatically cools. Isolated system (q = 0 and w = 0): ΔU = 0.","For a complete cycle, the system returns to its starting state, so ΔU = 0 and the net heat absorbed equals the net work done. This is why no machine can produce work continuously without an energy supply (a perpetual motion machine of the first kind is impossible).","The first law tells us the amount of energy involved in a change, but not whether the change is possible. For that, we need the second law."],"e":"A system absorbs 150 J of heat and does 60 J of work. ΔU = +150 + (−60) = 90 J."},"4.8":{"s":"Enthalpy H = U + PV. At constant pressure the heat exchanged equals the enthalpy change, so ΔH is what is measured in open vessels. For reactions of solids and liquids ΔH ≈ ΔU; when gases take part, ΔH and ΔU differ by Δn(gas) × RT, where Δn(gas) is the change in moles of gas.","f":[{"x":"H = U + P*V","n":"Definition of enthalpy; Delta H = Delta U + P Delta V at constant pressure.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.8, p.72"},{"x":"Delta H = Delta U + Delta n_g*R*T","n":"Relation for reactions involving gases; Delta n_g = moles of gaseous products - moles of gaseous reactants.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.8, p.73"}],"w":["Δn(gas) counts only gases; use R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ with T in kelvin and convert to kJ."],"x":["Most chemical reactions are carried out in open vessels, at constant (atmospheric) pressure, where the volume can change. It is useful to define a new function, the enthalpy, H = U + PV. It is a state function and it is extensive.","At constant pressure, the heat exchanged equals the change in enthalpy: q_p = ΔH. So a reaction with ΔH negative is exothermic (heat released), and one with ΔH positive is endothermic (heat absorbed). That is why the heats of reactions given in tables are enthalpy changes.","The link between ΔH and ΔU comes from ΔH = ΔU + PΔV. For reactions that involve gases, PΔV = Δn_g RT, so ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT, where Δn_g is the moles of gaseous products minus the moles of gaseous reactants. For reactions of solids and liquids only, ΔH is nearly equal to ΔU.","Examples: for H₂ + I₂ → 2HI, Δn_g = 0 and ΔH = ΔU. For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, Δn_g = −2, so ΔH is more negative than ΔU."],"e":"For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ at 300 K with ΔU = −91.0 kJ: ΔH = −91.0 + (−2)(8.314 × 10⁻³)(300) = −96.0 kJ."},"4.9":{"s":"Changes of state absorb or release heat at constant temperature. The enthalpy of fusion is the heat to melt one mole of a solid; the enthalpy of vaporisation is the heat to vaporise one mole of liquid; sublimation is solid straight to gas. Enthalpy of atomisation is the heat to break a substance into gaseous atoms.","x":["A change of physical state, such as melting or boiling, also takes in or gives out heat at constant pressure, and this is an enthalpy change. The temperature stays constant while it happens.","Enthalpy of fusion, Δ_fusH, is the heat absorbed when 1 mol of a solid melts at its melting point (for ice, 6.01 kJ/mol). Enthalpy of vaporisation, Δ_vapH, is the heat absorbed when 1 mol of a liquid turns into vapour at its boiling point (for water, 40.7 kJ/mol). Enthalpy of sublimation, Δ_subH, is the heat needed for 1 mol of a solid to change directly to vapour.","They are always positive, because each change needs energy to overcome the forces between the particles. Reverse changes (freezing, condensation) have the same size but the opposite sign. Because H is a state function, Δ_subH = Δ_fusH + Δ_vapH.","The enthalpy of vaporisation is much larger than the enthalpy of fusion, since vaporisation separates the molecules completely. This is why a burn from steam is worse than one from water at the same temperature: the steam gives out 40.7 kJ/mol as it condenses."],"e":"Melting 18 g (1 mol) of ice at 0 °C absorbs 6.01 kJ. Evaporating 18 g of water at 100 °C absorbs 40.7 kJ, about seven times as much."},"4.10":{"s":"Thermochemistry studies the heat of chemical reactions. A reaction is exothermic if ΔH is negative and endothermic if positive. Standard enthalpies refer to 1 bar, usually 298 K. Standard enthalpies of formation and combustion, and bond enthalpies, are used to find the enthalpy of a reaction. Hess's law: the total enthalpy change is the same by any route, so steps can be added.","w":["Reverse a reaction and ΔH changes sign; multiply a reaction and ΔH is multiplied too."],"x":["Thermochemistry deals with the heat changes in chemical reactions. A thermochemical equation shows the balanced reaction, the physical states, and the value of ΔH, for example C(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g), ΔH = −393.5 kJ. The value of ΔH applies to the amounts in the equation, so doubling the equation doubles ΔH, and reversing it changes the sign.","The standard state of a substance is its most stable pure form at 1 bar, and ΔH° is the change in standard conditions (usually 298 K). The standard enthalpy of formation, Δ_fH°, is the enthalpy change when 1 mol of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states. For an element in its standard state it is zero. The enthalpy of combustion is the heat given out when 1 mol of a substance burns completely in oxygen. It is always negative. The enthalpy of neutralisation of a strong acid by a strong base is about −57 kJ per mole of water, as the reaction is always H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O.","Hess's law says that the total enthalpy change for a reaction is the same whether it takes place in one step or in several, since H is a state function. We can add the equations of known reactions (reversing them or multiplying them as needed) to get the enthalpy of a reaction that cannot be measured directly.","From formation enthalpies, Δ_rH° = Σ Δ_fH°(products) − Σ Δ_fH°(reactants). From bond enthalpies, Δ_rH = Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed), which is only approximate, since bond enthalpies are averages."],"e":"C + O₂ → CO₂ has ΔH = −393.5 kJ and CO + ½O₂ → CO₂ has ΔH = −283.0 kJ. Subtracting gives C + ½O₂ → CO with ΔH = −110.5 kJ, which is hard to measure directly."},"4.11":{"s":"A spontaneous process runs by itself in one direction. Entropy S measures disorder; the total entropy (system plus surroundings) rises in a spontaneous change. Gibbs energy combines enthalpy and entropy: a process at constant temperature and pressure is spontaneous when ΔG is negative, at equilibrium when ΔG is zero.","f":[{"x":"Delta S = Q_rev/T","n":"Entropy change: heat absorbed reversibly divided by the temperature (J K^-1).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.11, p.82"},{"x":"Delta G = Delta H - T*Delta S","n":"Gibbs energy change at constant T and P; spontaneous if negative.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.11, p.84"},{"x":"T = Delta H/Delta S","n":"Temperature at which Delta G = 0 (equilibrium), assuming Delta H and Delta S do not change with T.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.11, p.84"},{"x":"Delta G = Delta G^0 + R*T*ln(Q)","n":"Gibbs energy change under non-standard conditions; Q = reaction quotient.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 4.11, p.84"}],"w":["ΔH negative and ΔS positive: spontaneous at all temperatures; ΔH positive and ΔS negative: never.","Compare ΔH with TΔS in the same units: kJ against J K⁻¹ × K."],"x":["A spontaneous process is one that takes place on its own, without outside help, once it has been started. Water flows downhill, heat flows from hot to cold, an iron nail rusts. The reverse never happens by itself. Spontaneous does not mean fast: the conversion of diamond to graphite is spontaneous but extremely slow.","Exothermic reactions often are spontaneous, but not always. Ice melts above 0 °C although melting needs heat. Something else is needed: entropy S, a measure of the disorder of the system. It rises when a solid melts, a liquid vaporises, a gas expands, or the number of gas molecules increases. The second law says that in a spontaneous process the total entropy of the system plus its surroundings increases.","To use this at constant temperature and pressure, we only need the system's quantities. The Gibbs energy is G = H − TS, and the change is ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. A process is spontaneous if ΔG is negative, at equilibrium if ΔG = 0, and non-spontaneous in the forward direction if ΔG is positive.","So there are four cases. ΔH negative and ΔS positive: always spontaneous. ΔH positive and ΔS negative: never. ΔH negative and ΔS negative: spontaneous at low temperature. ΔH positive and ΔS positive: spontaneous at high temperature, above T = ΔH/ΔS. The third law says the entropy of a perfect crystal is zero at 0 K."],"e":"ΔH = +30 kJ and ΔS = +100 J/K: ΔG = 0 at T = 30 000/100 = 300 K. The reaction is spontaneous above 300 K."}};
